Nearly all opinion polls taken before the first round of the election predicted President Komorowski would receive the largest vote share in the first round, with some polls taken in late 2014 and early 2015 suggesting he was on track for an outright win that would avoid a runoff election.<ref name="stunner">{{cite news|url=http://www.politico.eu/article/komorowski-loses-first-round-of-polish-elections-exit-poll-shows/|work=Politico|title=A Polish stunner|first=Jan|last=Cienski|date=10 May 2015|access-date=28 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-32862772|work=BBC News|title=Poland election: President Komorowski loses to rival Duda|date=25 May 2015|access-date=28 May 2015}}</ref>

Polls were closer in the second round, with most predicting a narrow Duda victory.<ref name="unpredictable">{{cite news|url=http://www.dw.de/komorowski-vs-duda-in-unpredictable-poland-runoff-poll/a-18472870|publisher=Deutsche Welle|title=Komorowski vs. Duda in unpredictable Poland runoff poll|date=24 May 2015|access-date=28 May 2015}}</ref> At least one late poll showed Komorowski pulling ahead, but just slightly.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-poland-vote-poll-idUKKBN0O70DM20150522|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181127001239/https://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-poland-vote-poll-idUKKBN0O70DM20150522|url-status=dead|archive-date=27 November 2018|work=Reuters|title=Poland's President Komorowski narrowly leads in poll ahead of run-off|date=22 May 2015|access-date=28 May 2015}}</ref>

